WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.240
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
On this episode,

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.490
we talked to doc.ai
about how they're

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.620
using Google Cloud for
their applications, chat

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.010
bots, and machine learning.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.160
So can you tell me
a little bit more

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.765
about what you actually
use inside of Google Cloud,

00:00:13.765 --> 00:00:16.140
for instance, the machine
learning and any other products

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.250
that you might use?

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.333
AKSHAY SHARMA: Definitely.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.090
So a lot of what we
build on the cloud

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.310
is mostly on the Google Cloud.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.400
So we use many different
services that Google offers.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.660
We try to be as Cloud
Native as we can.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.690
But there are some
important aspects

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.580
of Google Cloud that
makes our job easier

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.020
and gets to market sooner.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.410
So largely, our ecosystem of
tools that we use on Google

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.320
Cloud-- so we use GKE,
the Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.950
It's a fantastic product
from Google, in my opinion.

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.820
We build a lot of microservices
that are based on Docker.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.440
And then we are able
to rule that out

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.710
on GKE as multiple
nodes and pods.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.690
And it brings in
autoscaling aspects

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.350
for us, which is fantastic.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.430
So a lot of our data
collection microservices

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.490
are basically collecting
data across different apps

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.489
and users, but also
from our partners.

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:07.410
So one line of business
that doc.ai has is,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.780
we have licensed these
technologies to our partners.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.810
And they have millions
of users, from which we

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.410
are able to collect that
data that's coming in.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.170
So all the data
that's collected by

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.440
these different microservices,
which has burstable capacity,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.220
are actually stored on GCS,
the Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.740
So it's important
for us to understand

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:29.100
that Cloud Storage provides
the reliability that we need,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:32.340
because then we can use
multiple clouds, if we need,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.950
because it becomes a single
point of entry for us

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.570
to share data across
different clouds.

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:41.700
We also use many of the
databases that we have.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.130
We use, obviously,
Cloud SQL proxy,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:47.400
which makes the job of
being able to connect

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.260
to different services really
incredibly simple for us.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.650
The other thing that we have
done well on Google Cloud

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.050
is be able to build
parallelization,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:59.460
so that we can train many,
many jobs concurrently

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.470
or simultaneously,
so to speak, so we

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.380
can experiment with
different hyper-parameters.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.100
We use BigQuery for a lot of
our data analytics downstream.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.840
So based on how users are
collecting information,

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.050
especially around events
and other aspects,

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.303
we can throw it at
BigQuery and see

00:02:18.303 --> 00:02:20.220
what are some interesting
correlations that we

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:21.450
can see there.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.280
Most of our
machine-learning models

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:26.220
are built today
using TensorFlow.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.250
We have found that to be
production-ready from get-go,

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.950
which is something we
are very happy about.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.400
And I think it's also
easy to hire today,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.715
relatively in the world
of AI and deep learning--

00:02:38.715 --> 00:02:40.340
many of them are
actually being trained

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.140
on TensorFlow, which is a good
thing, with the help of what

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.240
Google is doing
across the ecosystem.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.000
We also use some of the other
specific hyper-specialized AI

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.720
modules you have.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.400
For example, Google
Vision API is

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.350
something we use for
extracting certain information

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.980
in the medical context,
specifically around pill

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.333
bottles and bounding boxes,
and things like that.

00:03:01.333 --> 00:03:03.750
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So you also
mentioned NLP a little while

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:04.250
ago.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.740
And then earlier, you
talked about the chat bots.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.270
How are users using chat bots?

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.008
And what is doc.ai
doing with those?

00:03:11.008 --> 00:03:12.550
AKSHAY SHARMA: It's
a great question.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:17.340
So one thing I did not
mention is that doc.ai also

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.380
has several chat bots.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.840
So we have a
direct-to-consumer chat bot,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.410
which is for mental health.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.220
So we have more than 600,000
users on the platform.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.150
It's a chat bot that runs
off of Facebook Messenger.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.670
What we have seen, the chat
bots is a very interesting

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.300
phenomena.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.690
Because for certain
types of diseases,

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.750
patients prefer talking
to a bot, as opposed

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.450
to a medical
professional, because one,

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.820
medical professional
are not necessarily

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.400
available in that area,
where they have these issues.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.520
And bots have no judgment.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.060
So they are not being watched
or being judged by someone.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.060
So we have mental
health chat bot.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.280
But we also work with
a lot of our partners

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.160
to rule out very
vertical specific chat

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.970
bots, for example, for
insurance companies,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:07.980
where we can do
member engagement.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.330
We are also building chat bots
for a very specific disease

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.170
verticals in the future.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:16.620
But what they do is
that they are somewhat

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.769
generalized enough, that now
we could take a model like that

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:22.980
and bring our own data and
throw our specific data

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.830
and train an improved
version of that,

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:29.610
that's somewhat focused in
the domain that we have.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.910
Of course, we need a lot
of data for it to improve.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:35.250
But then these models then
become really valuable,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.550
where you could engage them,
for example, in chat bots, where

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:42.360
you could predict the next
set of sequence of things

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.260
that a user may ask.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:46.150
So you can give them
better services right away.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.090
Or there is enough
contextual memory

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.340
of the previous
conversation, so you know

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:52.240
how to engage the user further.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:53.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thanks
so much for coming in,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.900
Akshay, and telling
us about how doc.ai

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.040
is working with the cloud.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:56.800
AKSHAY SHARMA: Thank you, Mark.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.870
This was really exciting.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.110
I'm very happy to
be here and hoping

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.270
to see many more talks in
the world of health care.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:05.900
That's important for
the general ecosystem,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.800
that Google is doing
a great job in.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.750
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:10.720
Thanks for watching.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.690
If you enjoyed this
conversation with doc.ai,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:15.023
check out the Stack Chat
playlist for more great videos.

00:05:15.023 --> 00:05:17.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

